# text = وذلك ان عملوا شى من امور الطب او امور الطبيعة بالاوزان فهكذى قد جرت عادتهم بالتقليب وذلك انه لا شى يشتركان فيه اوميروس وانفادقلس ما خلا الوزن ولذلك اما ذاك فينبغى ان نلقبه شاعرا واما هذا فالمتكلم فى الطبيعيات اكثر من الشاعر
# transliteration = wa-dhālika in ʿamilū shayʾan min umūri l-ṭibbi aw umūri l-ṭabīʿati bi-l-awzāni fa-hākadhā qad jarat ʿādatuhum bi-l-taqlībi wa-dhālika annahū lā shayʾa yashtarikāni fīhi Ūmīrūs wa-Anfādqlis mā khalā l-wazna wa-li-dhālika ammā dhāka fa-yanbaghī an nulaqqibahū shāʿiran wa-ammā hādhā fa-l-mutakallimu fī l-ṭabīʿiyyāti akthara mina l-shāʿiri
# prose_translation = For if they make something on matters of medicine or matters of nature in metres, this is how their custom of naming has run. Yet there is nothing that Homer and Empedocles share except the metre; therefore the former ought to be called a poet, while the latter is a speaker on natural things rather than a poet.
# literal_translation = And-that [is] if they-made thing of matters-of the-medicine or matters-of the-nature with-the-metres, so-thus indeed ran their-custom with-the-naming; and-that [is] that-it no thing they-two-share in-it, Homer and-Empedocles, except the-metre; and-for-that as-for that-one so-it-is-fitting that we-call-him poet, and-as-for this-one so-the-speaker in the-natural-things more than the-poet.
