# sent_id = 9.12
# document_id = 0086-034
# text_lang = ara
# subdoc = 9.12
# translation_lang = en
# text = وذلك ان التى هى عجيبة فهكذى فليكن حالها خاصة اكثر من تلك التى هى من تلقا نفسها ومن ما يتفق من قبل ان التى هى من الاتفاق قد يظن بها هى ايضا انها عجيبة اكثر بمبلغ ما ترى انها تكون دفعة بمنزلة حال التى لاندراس بن ميطياوس وهى انه كان قتل فى ارغوس لذلك الذى كان سبب مومٮٮه مياطياس عند ما راه وقد سقط وذلك انه يشتبه هى هذه الاشيا التى تجرى هذا المجرى انها ليس تكون باطلا ولا خرافا
# transliteration = wa-dhālika anna allatī hiya ʿajībatun fa-hākadhā fa-l-yakun ḥāluhā khāṣṣatan akthara min tilka allatī hiya min tilqāʾi nafsihā wa-min mā yattafiqu min qibali anna allatī hiya mina l-ittifāqi qad yuẓannu bihā hiya ayḍan annahā ʿajībatun akthara bi-mablaghi mā turā annahā takūnu dafʿatan bi-manzilati ḥāli allatī li-Andrās bni Mīṭiyāwus wa-hiya annahū kāna qatala fī Arghūs li-dhālika alladhī kāna sababa mawtihī(?) Miyāṭiyās ʿinda mā raʾāhu wa-qad saqaṭa wa-dhālika annahū yashtabihu hiya hādhihi l-ashyāʾu allatī tajrī hādhā l-majrā annahā laysa takūnu bāṭilan wa-lā khurāfan(?)
# prose_translation = For with what is marvellous, thus let its state be, especially and more than with what comes of its own accord and from what happens by chance; since what comes from chance is also thought to be the more marvellous to the extent that it is seen to come about all at once, like the case of that [statue] of Andras son of Mitys: namely that he had killed, in Argos, the one who had been the cause of his death (?), Mitys, when he saw him, having fallen. For these things that run this course seem not to come about in vain, nor as a fable (?).
# literal_translation = And-that [is] that which it [is] marvellous, so-thus so-let-be its-state especially more than that which it [is] from accord-of itself and-from what happens-by-chance; from account-of that which it [is] from the-chance, indeed is-thought of-it, it also, that-it [is] marvellous more, by-extent-of what it-is-seen that-it comes-about at-once; like-rank-of state-of which [is] to-Andras son-of Mitys, and-it [is] that-he had killed in Argos to-that who was cause-of his-death(?), Mitys, when saw-him and-indeed he-fell; and-that [is] that-it seems, it, these the-things which run this the-course, that-they not-are are vain and-not fable(?).
