# sent_id = 16.9
# document_id = 0086-034
# text_lang = ara
# subdoc = 16.9
# translation_lang = en
# text = واما الرابع فما يخطر بالفكر بمنزلة انه اتا من هو شبيه بالمكفنين لانسان ولم يات احد يشبه الا ارسطس فهذا اذا هو الذى اتا واما السوفسطانى فعند ما نظر فى لامور نظرا كثيرا قبل ايباغانيا على الحقيقة ظن بارسطس انه هو نفسه فكر ان اخته ذبحت وانه عرض انها انما كانت تضحى له وقال ايضا ثااودقطس انه عندما لسفينيدس من انها عند ما جات ونظرت الى الموضع فكرت فى باب القضا واخطر به ببالها انه جميعهن بهذا قضى عليهم ان يمتن وذلك انهم ٮٮحرحوں بالامر
# transliteration = wa-ammā l-rābiʿu fa-mā yakhṭuru bi-l-fikri bi-manzilati annahū atā man huwa shabīhun bi-l-mukaffanīna li-insānin wa-lam yaʾti aḥadun yushbihu illā Arisṭus fa-hādhā idhan huwa alladhī atā wa-ammā l-sūfisṭāniyyu fa-ʿinda mā naẓara fī l-umūri naẓaran kathīran qabla Ībāghāniyā ʿalā l-ḥaqīqati ẓanna bi-Arisṭus annahū huwa nafsuhū fakkara anna ukhtahū dhubiḥat wa-annahū ʿaraḍa annahā innamā kānat tuḍaḥḥī lahū wa-qāla ayḍan Thāʾūdqiṭus annahū ʿindamā li-Sfīnīdis min annahā ʿinda mā jāʾat wa-naẓarat ilā l-mawḍiʿi fakkarat fī bābi l-qaḍāʾi wa-akhṭara bihī bi-bālihā annahū jamīʿahunna bi-hādhā quḍiya ʿalayhim an yamutna wa-dhālika annahum ??ḥrḥūn(?) bi-l-amri
# prose_translation = As for the fourth, it is what comes to mind by thought: as [in the case] that someone came who resembles, among the shrouded ones, a man, and no one resembling [him] came except Orestes, so this one, then, is the one who came. As for the sophist, when he had looked into the matters at length before (?) Iphigenia, he supposed, in truth, of Orestes that he himself reflected that his sister had been slaughtered and that it befell that she was only sacrificing for him. And Theodectes also said that, regarding what is in the Phineidae: when she came and looked at the place, she reflected on the matter of fate, and it came to her mind that all of them were by this fated to die; for they […] (?) by the matter.
# literal_translation = And-as-for the-fourth, so-what occurs by-the-thought, like that-it came who he [is] resembling among-the-shrouded to-a-man, and-not came one resembles except Orestes; so-this then, it [is] that-which came. And-as-for the-sophist, so-when he-looked in the-matters looking much before Iphigenia, upon the-truth he-supposed of-Orestes that-he, he himself, reflected that his-sister was-slaughtered and-that-it befell that-she only was sacrificing for-him. And-said also Theodectes that-it [is] at-what [is] for-Phineidae: of that-they when she-came and-looked to the-place, she-reflected in chapter-of the-fate, and-brought it to-her-mind that-it all-of-them by-this was-decreed upon-them that they-die; and-that [is] that-they […](?) by-the-matter.
