# text = ولذلك كما قلنا وفرغنا من القول ڡـ*ل فليرا اوميروس فى هذا ذو سبه وناموس هدو من هذا الوجه ايضا يرى اوميروس انه متبع للناموس وانه لازم للصواب والاستقامة اكثر من هولا الاخر *هو الذى عمل الحرر وقد كان له اول واحد من حيث يرى ان ياتى به باسره هذا على انه قد كان عظيم جد ولم يكن يسهل رويته ولا ايضا كان مزمع ان يبين فى خرافته بهذه الحال من قبل انها قد كانت عند ما كانت تتركب وتقترن قد كانت تصغر فى عظمها والان فى هذه المداخل التى ٮقبصت جزو ما وهو ما الذى يفعل الانسان
# transliteration = wa-li-dhālika kamā qulnā wa-faraghnā mina l-qawli qablu(?) fa-l-yurā Ūmīrūs fī hādhā dhū sunnatin(?) wa-nāmūsin hdw min hādhā l-wajhi ayḍan yurā Ūmīrūs annahū muttabiʿun li-l-nāmūsi wa-annahū lāzimun li-l-ṣawābi wa-l-istiqāmati akthara min hāʾulāʾi l-ukhari *huwa alladhī ʿamila l-ḥrr(?) wa-qad kāna lahū awwalun wāḥidun min ḥaythu yarā an yaʾtiya bihī bi-asrihī hādhā ʿalā annahū qad kāna ʿaẓīman jiddan wa-lam yakun yashulu ruʾyatuhū wa-lā ayḍan kāna muzmiʿan an yabīna fī khurāfatihī bi-hādhihi l-ḥāli min qibali annahā qad kānat ʿinda mā kānat tatarakkabu wa-taqtarinu qad kānat taṣghuru fī ʿiẓamihā wa-l-āna fī hādhihi l-madākhili allatī taqabbaḍat(?) juzʾan mā wa-huwa mā alladhī yafʿalu l-insānu
# prose_translation = Therefore, as we have said and finished discussing before (?), let Homer be seen in this as a man of norm (?) and divine law […]; in this respect too Homer is seen to be a follower of the law and to hold to what is right and upright more than these others. He is the one who made the war (?), and it had a single beginning, inasmuch as he saw fit to bring it in its entirety; this although it was very great and its survey was not easy, nor again was it going to become clear in his tale in this state, because, when it was being composed and joined together, it was becoming smaller in its magnitude. And now [it is] in these episodes, which took hold of (?) a certain part, and that is what the man does …
# literal_translation = And-for-that, as we-said and-we-finished from the-speech before(?), so-let be-seen Homer in this possessor-of norm(?) and-law [...]; from this the-respect also is-seen Homer that-he [is] follower to-the-law and-that-he [is] adhering to-the-right and-the-uprightness more than these the-others. [...]-he [is] who made the-war(?), and-indeed was to-it beginning one, from where he-sees that he-bring it in-its-entirety; this upon that-it indeed was great very, and-not was easy its-seeing, and-not also was about-to that it-appear in his-tale in-this the-state, from account-of that-it indeed was, when was it-is-composed and-it-is-joined, indeed was it-becomes-small in its-magnitude. And-now in these the-episodes which took-hold(?) part some, and-it [is] what which does the-man.
