# sent_id = 25.16
# document_id = 0086-034
# text_lang = ara
# subdoc = 25.16
# translation_lang = en
# text = واما الذى ٮرٮدوں نحو المقولة فقد ينبغى ان يحل بمنزلة الكٮاڡ ما بلاورياس اولا ولعل ان يكون ليس يعنى بذلك البغال لكن يعنى الحفظة من قبل ان اورياس فى لغة اليونانى يدل على البغال وعلى الحفظة وايضا اذا ما قال انه قبيح المنظر ليس انما يعنى بذلك قبيح الوجه لكن عنا لا اعتدال البدن غير ان اهل قريطس يسمون الحسن المنظر للحسن الوجه ويسمون السكران المتقبل الوجه
# transliteration = wa-ammā alladhī yurīdūna(?) naḥwa l-maqūlati fa-qad yanbaghī an yuḥalla bi-manzilati l-kitāfi(?) mā bi-l-ūriyās awwalan wa-laʿalla an yakūna laysa yaʿnī bi-dhālika l-bighāla lākin yaʿnī l-ḥafaẓata min qibali anna ūriyās fī lughati l-yūnāniyyi yadullu ʿalā l-bighāli wa-ʿalā l-ḥafaẓati wa-ayḍan idhā mā qāla innahū qabīḥu l-manẓari laysa innamā yaʿnī bi-dhālika qabīḥa l-wajhi lākin ʿanā lā iʿtidāla l-badani ghayra anna ahla Qarīṭus yusammūna l-ḥasana l-manẓari li-l-ḥasani l-wajhi wa-yusammūna l-sakrāna l-mutaqabbila(?) l-wajhi
# prose_translation = As for what they intend (?) with regard to diction, it ought to be resolved as with the gloss (?), "with ourēas first": perhaps he does not mean by that the mules but the guards, since ourēas in the language of the Greeks signifies both the mules and the guards. Again, when he says that someone is ugly of appearance, he does not mean by that only ugly of face; rather he meant lack of proportion of the body; except that the people of Crete call "fair of appearance" the one fair of face, and they call the drunkard "mutaqabbil (?) of face".
# literal_translation = And-as-for which they-intend(?) toward the-diction, so-indeed it-is-fitting that be-resolved in-position-of the-?gloss what [is] with-ourēas first; and-perhaps that be not he-means by-that the-mules but he-means the-guards, from account-of that ourēas in language-of the-Greek signifies upon the-mules and-upon the-guards; and-also when he-said that-he [is] ugly-of the-appearance, not only he-means by-that ugly-of the-face, but he-meant non proportion-of the-body; except that people-of Crete name the-fair-of the-appearance for-the-fair-of the-face, and-they-name the-drunk the-?receiving-of the-face.
